21:03:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Rainbows are not formed only by the Sun. Any sufficiently bright light shining on water droplets can make a rainbow, including the Moon, a spotlight, or sometimes street/building lights. The Sun is just the most common source because it is: - **Very bright**, so the rainbow is visible even against the bright daytime sky. - **Broad-spectrum (“white”) light**, containing the range of colors that droplets separate. - **Effectively far away**, so its rays arrive nearly parallel. This produces the familiar, well-defined circular rainbow geometry. For a rainbow, light must enter raindrops, reflect once inside them, and leave at about **42° from the direction directly opposite the light source** (for the main rainbow). So if a streetlamp is behind you and rain or mist is in front of you, a rainbow can appear around the point opposite the lamp. Artificial-light rainbows are uncommon because streetlights are usually: - **Not bright enough** compared with surrounding lights or the dark sky, - **Too close or spread out**, which can blur the bow, - **Not white-spectrum**—for example, some LED or sodium lamps have limited spectral colors, so the rainbow may look weak, oddly colored, or nearly monochrome. You can sometimes see artificial rainbows in mist from sprinklers, fountains, or waterfalls when illuminated by a bright lamp or car headlights. Moonlight can also make a “moonbow,” though it often looks white to our eyes because moonlight is faint.
