21:04:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That makes sense—an ordinary flashlight-and-hose setup is usually just too dim to produce a **visible colored** rainbow, even though the same optical process is happening. Sunlight on the spray is enormously brighter than a typical flashlight beam. A rainbow is already faint: only a small fraction of the light entering each droplet is redirected toward your eyes. In daylight, the Sun supplies enough light for the separated colors to stand out. At night, the weak light may produce only a dim whitish glow, or nothing noticeable. Also, at low light levels your eyes rely more on rod cells, which are very sensitive to light but poor at seeing color. This is why moonbows usually look white or gray, despite physically containing the same spectrum of colors. Geometry matters too. To try it at night: - Use a **very bright, broad-spectrum white light**—a powerful LED flashlight, spotlight, or car headlights work better than a small household flashlight. - Put the light **behind or very near your head**, pointed into the mist. - Look at fine spray **in front of you**, especially around roughly 40–42° away from the direction you are looking straight ahead. - Use a dark background and fine mist rather than a heavy stream of water. With enough illumination, artificial rainbows can absolutely be made from sprinkler mist or fountain spray. Your daylight hose rainbow is proof that the droplets are suitable; the Sun is mainly winning because it is vastly brighter than your flashlight.
