21:04:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m very sure about the physics: **water droplets do not care whether the incoming light came from the Sun, a lamp, or a projector.** Refraction and internal reflection work the same way. But your intuition about not seeing many night-time examples is reasonable, because the usual conditions are much harder to meet than they sound. A visible rainbow needs more than “light + water”: - The light needs to be **bright at the droplets**, not merely bright-looking from where you stand. - It needs enough **different wavelengths** for distinct colors. Older sodium streetlights, for example, can be nearly yellow-orange, so they cannot make a normal multicolored bow. - The source must be roughly **behind the observer**, while the mist/rain is in front. - The source should be near the observer’s line of sight or far enough away that its illumination direction is nearly uniform. - The water needs to be a relatively fine, dense spray against a suitably dark background. Car headlights often fail the geometry and illumination test in practice. They shine low and forward, rain is distributed irregularly, the road and other lights create glare, and the headlights are separated from your eyes. A bow may be faint, incomplete, distorted, or washed out rather than a dramatic sky-spanning arc. Stadium lights can be extremely bright, but they are usually high above and off to one side, not behind your head. They can illuminate rain spectacularly without placing the rainbow in a convenient part of your view. Artificial rainbows are nevertheless well established and routinely produced in demonstrations using a strong white spotlight or projector shining through mist or spray. They are also sometimes photographed in fountains, waterfalls, sprinkler spray, caves, and industrial mist. They are just not common enough—or visually dramatic enough—to become the iconic “rainbow after rain” image. A good way to frame it is: - **Sun + rain** is the easy, large-scale, familiar case. - **Artificial light + mist** can make a rainbow too, but it generally requires a much more controlled setup. - A weak flashlight may physically send rainbow-separated light toward you, but it is often below the threshold where your eyes can see the colored arc.
